  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nTHUNDERSTORM CHANCES:\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON--TONIGHT...While an additional isolated \nshower/thunderstorm can't be ruled out over southern Kansas this \nafternoon-evening, thinking subsidence in wake of this morning's \nconvection should keep a lid on most thunderstorm chances the rest \nof the day and through tonight.\n\nSUNDAY LATE AFTERNOON--MONDAY EVENING...A cold front approaching \nfrom the northwest may support an isolated high-based thunderstorm \nor two generally along/north of I-70 late Sunday and again late \nMonday, but weak forcing should keep activity quite isolated, so \nmost locations should remain thunderstorm-free. The main hazard \nwould likely be isolated strong to severe wind gusts given inverted-\nv thermodynamic profiles.\n\nLATE TUESDAY--WEEK'S END...Per deterministic and ensemble consensus, \na shortwave progressing across Canada and the northern CONUS is \nexpected to progress a cold front south into the region toward mid-\nweek, and stalling it over Mid-America through at least week's end. \nA separate shortwave a few days later is expected to progress \nanother cold front south, acting to reinforce the aforementioned \nstalled frontal. The result should be off-and-on thunderstorm \nchances from late Tuesday through at least week's end, and possibly \neven into next weekend. These isolated to scattered thunderstorm \nchances will likely be highest each day from the early evening hours \nthrough the night, and dissipating in the morning. While widespread \nsevere weather is not expected, a seasonable buoyancy/shear \ncombination should support at least an isolated severe thunderstorm \nthreat each day/night during this period.\n\nTEMPERATURES AND WIND:\n\nSATURDAY--WEDNESDAY...Persistent central/southern CONUS upper \nridging in concert with breezy/gusty south winds will likely support \nabove average temperatures through at least mid-week. Low-level \nthickness values supports widespread upper 90s and 100s during the \nday, with overnight lows likely no cooler than the upper 70s to low \n80s. Forecast highs may actually be a tad conservative (especially \nalong/west of I-135) given the mostly dry weather and associated \nlower soil moisture the past several weeks. Two saving graces during \nthis upcoming heat wave are the likelihood of lower dewpoints \ncompared to the July heat waves and stout/gusty south winds, which \nwill aid in evaporative cooling effects on skin. Heat indices around \n100-107 degrees are likely, highest over southern/southeast KS, so \nlater shifts may need to consider Heat Advisory headlines at some \npoint.\n\nTHURSDAY--NEXT WEEKEND...A frontal zone oozing south and stalling \nsomewhere over Mid-America should support somewhat cooler \ntemperatures as we head into late week and beyond. The degree of \ncooling is highly uncertain and will be tied to how far south the \nfrontal zone can reach. Cooler scenarios support widespread daytime \nhighs in the 80s (especially next weekend), while warmer scenarios \nonly \"cool off\" the region into the 90s. Stay tuned as we refine \nforecast details in the coming days.",
